Changed v10.0.0: The `'readable'` is always emitted in the next tick after `.push()` is called.
Changed v10.0.0: Using `'readable'` requires calling `.read()`.

Event: `'resume'`
The 'resume' event is emitted when stream.resume() is called and readableFlowing is not true.
Added: v0.9.4

`readable.destroy([error])`
Destroy the stream. Optionally emit an 'error' event, and emit a 'close' event (unless emitClose is set to false). After this call, the readable stream will release any internal resources and subsequent calls to push() will be ignored. 
Once destroy() has been called any further calls will be a no-op and no further errors except from _destroy() may be emitted as 'error'. 
Implementors should not override this method, but instead implement readable._destroy().
Added: v8.0.0
Changed v14.0.0: Work as a no-op on a stream that has already been destroyed.

